content.text:
Now desk tag y 539.2-552.1; umbrella tag y 544.7-557.6 → overlap vertically 7.4. So core desk tag above dot (dot center? desk button top likely 539+1? Let's compute: side top tag bottom = button top-1 = 552.1 → button top = 553.1. Desk dot center = 553.1+23 = 576.1. Umbrella tag center = 551.15 → its dot center = 551.15+9 = 560.15. So umbrella dot is 16px above desk dot. Side tag spans 544.7-557.6, core desk tag spans 539.2-552.1; they overlap 7.4. Right: side tag at dot-9 center is *higher* than core tag top? Core tag above dot spans dot-36..-24 = 540..552; side tag spans extra dot-15..-3 = 545..557. Overlap 545-552.

So for a core marker whose tag is above, side tags near that dot (slightly above) collide. Instead side tags should be at dot center (previous) for these; but that collided with core *below* tags. There's no vertical position that avoids both when extra dot is within ~20px of core dot. Need horizontal-only solution: side tag shouldn't extend into core tag's horizontal span. The core tag is centered on core dot, width ~28. Extra is 56 scene units to the side = 32px at 390, so extra dot center is 32px from core, its side tag extending toward core by 15px gap+8 offset... Currently `right: calc(50% + 8px)` puts the tag's right edge 8px left of extra dot center → 24px from core dot; core tag half-width 14px, so gap 10px. Yet measured overlap horizontally 15.6 for umbrella (w 47). Wait tag x 69.3-116.5; core tag 100.8-129.2 → overlap 15.7. Extra dot center = x tag right +8? For align right, tag left = button center +8. Button center ≈? tag left 69.3 → button center 61.3? That can't be: umbrella st x=60 scene units → screen x ≈ 60/400 * 390 ≈ 58.5 + art left (~16) = 74.5. Hmm tag left 69.3 → button center = 61.3? Maybe button left 39? Let me compute: for align right tag left = button center + 8 → center = 61.3, so button left = 39.3. But umbrella scene x=60 → 60*0.975 + 16 = 74.5. Discrepancy: maybe art left 0 and scene width 390 → 58.5. Button center should be 58.5. Tag left would be 66.5. Close to 69.3. Core desk x=116 → 113.1. Core tag centered at 113.1 with w 28.4 → 98.9-127.3, matches 100.8-129.2 (offset 2). Extra tag 69.3-116.5 ends at 116.5, core tag starts 100.8 → overlap 15.7. So extra side tag extends to 116.5, which is *right of* the extra dot (69.3 + 47.2 = 116.5; extra dot center ~74.5?). It should extend right, so it spans from dot+8 to dot+55. That reaches toward the core dot at 113. Oops! For a right-aligned extra to the LEFT of the desk core, the label extending right collides with the core label. The desk extra left has align 'left' though! Why is umbrella (slot 0, align left) showing align right? Because edge-flip: pt.x=60/400=15% <22 → flipped to right. That's the bug: flipping left-aligned to right because near left edge, but then the label extends toward the core object. In the earlier config (dx -46), x=70 (17.5%) also flipped.

For near-edge objects to the left of the core, the label should still extend left (or up/down), not right toward the core. Edge clipping is less bad than overlapping? We clamp x≥32 = 8%, maybe with a shorter tag it fits. At 390 art: x=31px screen; label width 47px extending left → negative. So left tag would clip. Alternative for edge cases: keep the label below/above the dot (centered) rather than side. That avoids both clipping and core collision if vertical separation is enough.

Simplest robust solution: don't use side labels at all for these tiny screens? But 390 is the primary size and had zero overlaps with centered/above tags before (except artificial test). Wait the first test before side labels at 390 had BAD 0 and OVERLAPS 0 (with all 9 stations!? That test lacked slot but used centered tags). Yes! At 390, centered tags produced zero overlaps across all scenes. At 320/568, centered produced overlaps 25/34 (mostly small). So centered tags are fine at 390, side labels introduce collisions.

Maybe revert side alignment entirely and accept small overlaps at 320 widths? But the original 8-object screenshot at 390 showed labels clustered on desk (they didn't overlap per measurement, but visually were adjacent). The screenshot: TABLE + two dots with labels likely hidden behind? It looked messy partly because of labels but measurement says no overlap. Hmm.

Better approach: use side alignment ONLY when it moves away from the core tag's center, i.e., based on relative position to core station, not edge. For desk extras: left extra align left, right extra align right (good—away from core). But if left extra is near left edge, align left tag clips. In that case, center it (no align) rather than flip right. Clipping vs collision: we clamp x≥32 scene units; at 390, tag width 47px, x=31px → extends to -16px (clipped 16px, ugly). Could increase clamp to x≥48 (12%): then label left extent 47-8-? Dot at 47px, tag right edge at dot-8=39, left=8 → fits! And art object at x=48 is still visible (halfwidth ~17 units). At 320 art width 225: dot at 27px, tag width 47 (font smaller 6px → maybe 42) → left = 27-8-42=-23 → clipped. Hmm at 320, tags are fixed pixel widths while art shrinks, so edge items need centered tags.

Could add a media query for max-width 370 (or max-height 500) to keep tags centered? Then collisions return at 320 but only small overlaps (4-12px area) and mostly vertical. Not ideal.

Alternative for edge items: side label but clamp horizontal shift: use `right:auto; left:50%; transform:translateX(-100%)` etc. Same clipping.

Better robust approach: dynamically choose label placement among four positions (above, below, left, right) per hotspot, avoiding overlap with other labels and staying in bounds. We can compute after layout in JS and assign data-side/align. That's more work but doable and would look polished. However, labels are 8; we can implement a simple post-layout pass:

1. Render all hotspots centered below (default).
2. For each hotspot in order, choose placement from ['bottom','top','left','right'] that:
   - keeps tag within art bounds,
   - minimizes overlap area with already placed tags (and maybe with dots?).
3. Apply data-side/align accordingly.

This adapts to all viewports and scenes. Need to run after renderCase + fonts loaded. Tag sizes unknown until measured; can measure after setting text and appending. Do a `layoutHotspotTags()` called after hotspots appended (requestAnimationFrame) and on resize. To measure candidate positions, temporarily set attributes and read rects; 8 objects × 4 positions = 32 measurements per layout, fine.

But side alignment CSS needs left tag and right tag; top/bottom already. Implement candidates as classes/attrs: side 'bottom', 'top', 'left', 'right'. CSS:
- left/right as side tags centered at top 50%.
- top/bottom as existing.

Algorithm:
- Get artRect.
- For each button in order, get dot rect.
- For each placement, set data-side/align accordingly, force reflow? Use getBoundingClientRect of tag after setting attr; browser reflows synchronously on read.
- Compute tag rect for candidate.
- Score: penalty 10000 if outside art bounds; else overlap area with placed tag rects (plus maybe distance).
- Choose min score; apply.
- Mark tag rect placed.

Since labels may overlap object art, acceptable.

Also add small buffer: expand placed rects by 2px; count overlap area.

Call after appending hotspots, inside requestAnimationFrame to ensure fonts/styles. Also call on resize (after fitSceneTitle). The tag sizes change with media queries on resize, so re-run.

Would this eliminate manual align logic? Yes. Remove side assignment in renderCase (just text/labels, maybe slot not needed). Keep data-align CSS for left/right.

Placement candidate mapping:
- bottom: data-side=bottom, no align
- top: data-side=top, no align
- left: data-align=left
- right: data-align=right

