content.text:
Now implement JS:

1. Add `REVEAL_LINES` constant near SEARCH_FLAVOR maybe.
2. Add `PROBE_ACTION` mapping/helper.
3. Add `FIND_ICONS` constant.
4. Add `plan.revealed`, `plan.revealLine`.
5. Modify renderZoom to show revealed state and render token.
6. Refactor position math.
7. Modify renderSearchSpots to add has-find.
8. Modify showSpotResult / searchSpot.
9. Add `renderFindToken`, `placeFindToken`, `probeSpot`, `collectClue`.
10. Event delegation on #zoomFinds.
11. Resize renderZoom handles token.
12. Expose collectClue maybe for tests.

Let's write carefully.

### Constants

Near `SEARCH_FLAVOR`:
```
const REVEAL_LINES = [
  '{place}: something hidden, deliberately out of sight.',
  '{place}: your fingers catch an edge. It does not belong to the room.',
  '{place}: a shape in the dust, tucked where nobody would look.'
];
```
But placeholder `{place}` should be phrase with lowercase; we cap. We can store `replaceAll('{place}', cap(spotPhrase(...)))`.

### Probe actions

```
const PROBE_ACTION_GROUPS = [
  ['wipe', ['TABLETOP', ...]],
  ...
];
const PROBE_ACTION_BY_LABEL = {};
... build map.
function probeActionFor(label) { return PROBE_ACTION_BY_LABEL[label] || 'lift'; }
```
Could use regex:
```
function probeActionFor(label){
 const s = label.toUpperCase();
 if (/(DRAWER|TRAY|TILL|POCKET|LINING|SLEEVE|CUFF|HEM|HOOD|COLLAR|SHELF|PAGE)/.test(s)) return 'open';
 if (/(DIAL|LOCK|HINGE|LATCH|HANDLE|KNOB|CLASP|STRIKER|PRIMER|CHAIN|RING|FOB|TEETH|SPINE)/.test(s)) return 'turn';
 if (/(CORD|STRAP|STRING|RAIL|BAR|THRESHOLD|STEP|TRANSOM|SLAT|BARS|DOOR)/.test(s)) return 'pull';
 if (/(TABLETOP|BAR TOP|COUNTER|BENCH|FELT|GLASS|PANE|MIRROR|LABEL|LENS|CHART|FACE|BACK|RIM|EDGE)/.test(s)) return 'wipe';
 return 'lift';
}
```
Order matters: "BREAST POCKET" matches POCKET, good. "TOOL DRAWER" matches DRAWER. "POWDER TRAY" matches TRAY. "BOOK REST" default lift. "LETTER TRAY" open. "SILL" default lift. "FLOOR"? none. "KICK PLATE" default lift. Fine.
Note `'BAR TOP'` contains 'BAR' which would match pull if pull checked before wipe; so check wipe before pull or regex `RAIL` only. Move wipe before pull. Good.

### Find icons

```
const FIND_ICONS = {
 note: `<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" ...><path d="M6 3h9l3 3v15H6z".../><path d="M9 10h7M9 13h7M9 16h4".../></svg>`,
 gun: `<svg ...><rect x="8" y="4" width="6" height="13" rx="1.2"/><path d="M10 17h8l-1.5 3H10z"/></svg>`,
 weapon: `<svg ...><path d="M4 19l12-13 4 3.5L9 21z"/><path d="M14 5l4 3.5"/></svg>`,
 alibi: `<svg ...><path d="M4 7h16v10H4z"/><path d="M9 7v10M15 7v10"/></svg>`,
 red: `<svg ...><path d="M8 4h8v5l-4 2-4-2z"/>?` maybe lipstick: `<rect x="8" y="11" width="8" height="9" rx="1"/><path d="M9 11V8l3-4 3 4v3"/>`
};
```
All stroke currentColor, fill none, stroke-width 1.5, linecap round.

Maybe use simpler:
- note: sheet with folded corner and lines.
- gun: bullet casing.
- weapon: knife blade.
- alibi: ticket.
- red: lipstick.
No text.

### Position math

Current `placeSearchSpots` computes all. We can add a shared helper:

```
function spotPixel(plan, index, frame, layerBox, bayBox) {
  const spot = plan.spots[index];
  const scale = Math.max(bayBox.width / frame.w, bayBox.height / frame.h);
  const offsetX = bayBox.left - layerBox.left + (bayBox.width - frame.w * scale) / 2;
  const offsetY = bayBox.top - layerBox.top + (bayBox.height - frame.h * scale) / 2;
  const px = frame.box.x + spot.u * frame.box.w;
  const py = frame.box.y + spot.v * frame.box.h;
  return {
    x: offsetX + (px - frame.x) * scale,
    y: offsetY + (py - frame.y) * scale
  };
}
```
But layerBox is relative to each layer; both layers same size/position (absolute inset 0). So we can use zoomSpots layer box for all, or zoomFinds. Pass layer.

Update `placeSearchSpots`:
```
const pos = spotPixel(plan,i,frame,layerBox,bayBox);
left clamp(pos.x,23,width-23)
```
For find token: same, clamp 30 perhaps.

`placeFindToken(plan, frame)`:
```
const layer = $('#zoomFinds');
const token = $('.clue-find', layer);
if (!token || !plan) return;
...
const pos = spotPixel(...);
const x = clamp(pos.x, 30, layerBox.width - 30);
const y = clamp(pos.y, 30, layerBox.height - 34);
token.style.left=...
token.style.top=...
token.dataset.tag = y > layerBox.height - 54 ? 'top' : 'bottom';
```
But `data-tag` values expected "top" or default; CSS uses `[data-tag="top"]`. Set bottom otherwise. The tag width maybe 30px; clamping x to 30 leaves half tag ~15 inside. Good.
What if the token is near the top and the blowout tag below; fine.

We should also clamp to avoid the clue sheet? The art layer ends above the sheet, so fine.

### renderFindToken

```
function findIconFor(type) { return FIND_ICONS[type] || FIND_ICONS.note; }

function renderFindToken(key, plan, frame) {
  const layer = $('#zoomFinds');
  if (!layer) return;
  layer.innerHTML = '';
  if (!plan?.revealed || state.found.has(key)) return;
  const spot = plan.spots[plan.clueSpot];
  const meta = stationOf(state.caseData.location, key);
  const btn = document.createElement('button');
  btn.type='button';
  btn.className='clue-find';
  btn.dataset.spot=String(plan.clueSpot);
  btn.innerHTML = `<span class="find-halo"></span><span class="find-card">${FIND_ICONS[state.caseData.clues[key].type]||FIND_ICONS.note}</span><span class="find-tag">TAKE</span>`;
  btn.setAttribute('aria-label', `Take the clue hidden ${spotPhrase(spot.label, meta)}`);
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => collectClue(key));
  layer.appendChild(btn);
  placeFindToken(plan, frame);
}
```
But if we call `renderZoom` multiple times, layer resets. Good.
The button focus call: maybe not on initial render if the reveal just happened? `revealClue` calls renderFindToken then focus. `renderZoom` on screen open should not steal focus. Add optional `focusToken` param or call separately in `revealClue`.
- In `renderZoom`, after `renderSearchSpots`, call `renderFindToken(key, plan, closeup.frame)`.
- On `revealClue`, after `showSpotResult`, call `renderFindToken`, then `requestAnimationFrame(() => $('.clue-find')?.focus())` to guide. But maybe focus is good.

### Update renderSearchSpots

After `if (found && i === plan.clueSpot) btn.classList.add('is-clue');`
Add:
```
if (!found && plan.revealed && i === plan.clueSpot) btn.classList.add('has-find');
```
Also if found, no has-find.

### renderZoom sheet

Current:
```
$('#clueText').textContent = found ? clue.text : `Work...`;
$('#clueSheet').classList.toggle('is-gun', found && clue.type==='gun');
$('#clueSheet').classList.toggle('is-searching', !found);
...
status ... found ? logged : searched.size ? ... : ...
```
Update:
```
const revealed = !found && plan.revealed;
$('#clueText').textContent = found ? clue.text : revealed ? plan.revealLine : prompt;
$('#clueSheet').classList.toggle('is-searching', !found && !revealed);
$('#clueSheet').classList.toggle('is-revealed', revealed);
$('#clueSheet').classList.toggle('is-gun', found && clue.type==='gun');
...
status.classList.toggle('ok', found);
status.classList.toggle('find', revealed);
status.textContent = found ? '✓ LOGGED...' : revealed ? 'CLUE FOUND · TAP TO TAKE' : ...
```
`is-searching` italic style; revealed has its own italic style.
`#zoomFound` unchanged.

Then after render closeup and `renderSearchSpots`, call `renderFindToken`. The `zoomMeta` set before? Current order:
```
const bay...
const closeup...
$('#zoomCanvas').innerHTML...
state.zoomMeta = {...}
$('#zoomMag')...
renderSearchSpots(key, plan, closeup.frame);
```
Call `renderFindToken(key, plan, closeup.frame)` after renderSearchSpots. Note `renderSearchSpots` schedules `layoutSearchSpots`, and renderFindToken positions immediately; the bay has final text. Good.

### searchSpot

New behavior:
```
function searchSpot(index) {
  ...
  const wasRevealed = plan.revealed && !state.found.has(key);
  if (index === plan.clueSpot) {
    if (state.found.has(key)) {
      // review
      Sfx.tick();
      showSpotResult(index);
      return;
    }
    if (wasRevealed) {
      collectClue(key);
      return;
    }
    // first search of clue spot: reveal only
    searched.add(index); // already done
    const btn... dataset searched
    mark .has-find
    plan.revealed = true;
    plan.revealLine = buildRevealLine(...)
    probeSpot(key, index);
    Sfx.discover();
    showSpotResult(index); // this handles revealed
    renderFindToken(key, plan, state.zoomMeta.frame);
    announce(`Something hidden ${place}. Tap the finding to take the clue.`)
    focus token
    return;
  }
  // false lead
  ...
}
```
Careful ordering of `searched.add` and `firstLook`. We currently add before. We'll restructure.

Maybe:
```
const wasSearched = searched.has(index);
searched.add(index);
const btn...
if (index === plan.clueSpot && !state.found.has(key)) {
  if (!plan.revealed) {
    plan.revealed = true;
    plan.revealLine = buildRevealLine...
    btn?.classList.add('has-find');
    probeSpot(key, index);
    Sfx.discover();
    showSpotResult(index);
    renderFindToken(...)
    announce(...)
    requestAnimationFrame(() => $('.clue-find')?.focus({preventScroll:true}));
    return;
  }
  collectClue(key);
  return;
}
if (index === plan.clueSpot) { // found
  btn?.classList.add('is-clue');
  Sfx.tick();
  showSpotResult(index);
  return;
}
// false
Sfx.tick();
probeSpot(key,index);
if (!wasSearched) announce(...)
showSpotResult(index);
```
But if plan.revealed and the player taps the token, collectClue called directly, not searchSpot. If they tap the spot pip (token covers, but keyboard), searchSpot sees revealed and collects. Good.
- When collecting via token, ensure the spot is marked searched and `has-find` removed; `renderSearchSpots` already marked searched (when revealed). Good.
- If somehow token clicked without searched, fine.

- For false search, `probeSpot` should run even if already searched? It gives feedback; yes. But may be excessive; okay.

`showSpotResult`:
- If found -> clue text, status logged, is-gun.
- Else if plan.revealed -> revealed sheet text/status (not the spot's result). `showSpotResult` currently sets false lead text. Need modify:
```
const revealed = !found && plan.revealed;
sheet.classList.toggle('is-searching', !found && !revealed);
sheet.classList.toggle('is-revealed', revealed);
if (found) { text=clue.text; status logged }
else if (revealed) { text=plan.revealLine; status 'CLUE FOUND · TAP TO TAKE'; ... }
else { text=spot.text; status `SEARCHING...`; }
```
- `status.classList.toggle('ok', found)`, `.find`, etc.
- For revealed, `sheet.is-gun` false (not collected).
- `zoomFound` same.

But when `showSpotResult` is called for a false spot *after* the clue is already revealed (e.g., player searches another spot), it will show the revealed message, not the false spot text. Is that desirable? The clue token is on screen and the sheet says take it. The player tapped another spot; showing the revealed message is okay, but they lose the false lead result. Better: the revealed clue should remain the priority. But then false spot interactions after reveal don't show text. They can collect first, then search others. That's acceptable. Alternatively, show false lead text but status "CLUE FOUND · TAP TO TAKE" and keep token; but then the sheet is not clearly directing. Hmm.

The user wants discovery; once revealed, the clue is the focus. I'd keep the revealed message. The false spot pip still dims and status could say "DRAWER · NOTHING CONNECTED · CLUE STILL WAITING". But simpler to show revealed. We'll do that. Actually, to avoid confusion when the player taps a false spot after reveal, we can show the false spot result but with status "CLUE FOUND · TAP THE FINDING" maybe. Let's not overcomplicate.

- `searchSpot` for false after reveal will call showSpotResult → revealed message. Fine.

`collectClue(key)`:
```
function collectClue(key) {
  if (!key || state.found.has(key)) return;
  const plan = getSearchPlan(key);
  if (!plan?.revealed) return;
  const clue = state.caseData.clues[key];
  state.found.add(key);
  plan.revealed = true;
  renderSceneState();
  Sfx.clue();
  const spotBtn = $(`#zoomSpots .search-spot[data-spot="${plan.clueSpot}"]`);
  spotBtn?.classList.remove('has-find');
  spotBtn?.classList.add('is-clue');
  const token = $('.clue-find');
  if (token) {
    token.classList.add('is-taken');
    setTimeout(() => token.remove(), 280);
  }
  showSpotResult(plan.clueSpot);
  cameraFlash(0.5);
  announce(`Clue logged: ${clue.text}`);
  requestAnimationFrame(() => spotBtn?.focus({preventScroll:true}));
}
```
- `showSpotResult` uses `state.screen`? no, but `state.zoomMeta` to reposition; fine.
- `renderSceneState` updates hotspots; if the scene screen isn't active, still okay.
- The token removal after 280ms; if renderZoom happens in between, fine.
- `cameraFlash` adds a flash overlay globally; nice.
- The status becomes logged and text full clue. Good.
- The `#zoomFound` updates.
- If the token is focused and removed, focus falls back to body; we focus spot in rAF after 280? We focus immediately, but token removal later doesn't matter because focus is on spot. Good.
- `plan.revealed` stays true.

`buildRevealLine(plan)`:
```
function revealLineFor(spot, meta) {
  const line = pick(REVEAL_LINES).replaceAll('{place}', cap(spotPhrase(spot.label, meta)));
  return line;
}
```
Store `plan.revealLine = ...`.

`probeSpot(key,index)`:
```
let probeTimer = 0;
function probeSpot(key,index){
  const plan = getSearchPlan(key);
  const spot = plan?.spots[index];
  if (!spot) return;
  const station = $(`#zoomCanvas .closeup-svg [data-station="${key}"]`);
  if (station) {
    station.classList.remove('probe-open', ...);
    void station.getBoundingClientRect();
    station.classList.add(`probe-${probeActionFor(spot.label)}`);
    clearTimeout(probeTimer);
    probeTimer = setTimeout(() => station.classList.remove(...), 650);
  }
  const layer = $('#zoomFinds');
  const frame = state.zoomMeta?.frame;
  if (!layer || !frame) return;
